{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nThe code-review `tests` overlay (blocking at 0.54 —\n`src/rebar/llm/code_review/criteria_routing.json:110-121`) is selected only when a changed file\nmatches its `applies_to` globs `**/test_*.py`, `**/*_test.py`, `**/tests/**`\n(`registry.glob_triggered_overlays`, `registry.py:340-356`). A change adding product logic and no\ntest file never runs it unless the base reviewer happens to recommend it, and guard (vii)\n(`code-review-tests.md:97`) keeps a bare coverage-gap finding advisory.\n\nFour changes adding declarations merged on `LLM-Review +1` with zero test files: Gerrit 388\n(`d9e0f0e733`, 15 added `def`/`class` lines, new `git_adapter.py`), 1920 (`721cb7d6ac`, 3), 1737\n(`1c2bf6e57b`, 22), 1703 (`83d91d488c`, 8). 388 later produced three bugs (`79c6-eaf3-38af-4ce9` +\ntwo siblings) — the `git_adapter` by-path-load family the epic names. Gerrit 1547 (`24917e843b`) is\na NEGATIVE case: comment-only source delta, 0 added declarations. Approved in the 2026-08-21\nfortnight defect review; diff-coverage was rejected — this project tests behaviour, not coverage %.\n\n## Approach\nFire the overlay from the diff CONTENT, where the diff and the changed files are both known.\n1. `content_triggered_overlays` (`registry.py:461`) takes `(diff_text, repo_root)` only and is\n   path-blind by construction (every helper skips the `+++`/`---` headers), so the \"no test file\n   changed\" half of the rule has no operand there. Add a keyword-only\n   `changed_files: Sequence[str] = ()` (call sites stay positional) and pass the step's `changed`\n   list at `workflow_ops.py:99`.\n2. The detector is a NEW module-level helper (`_has_added_declaration`), NOT extra branches inside\n   `content_triggered_overlays`: the complexity ratchet fails on `new>0` and may not be raised. It\n   fires when, for some changed file, an ADDED (`+`) line matches\n   the EXISTING polyglot `_REMOVED_DECL_RE` (`registry.py:399-412` — Python `def`/`class`, Go\n   `func`, Rust `fn`, JS/TS `function`/arrow/`class`) and the NAME it declares is not also removed\n   on a `-` line in that same file (per-file provenance from the `diff --git … b/<path>` headers,\n   since the composed context interleaves files). `_REMOVED_DECL_RE` has no capture group, so the name comes from a\n   SEPARATE regex over the matched line, spanning the same keywords (`def`/`class`/`fn`/`func`/\n   `function`, `const`/`let`/`var`). NAMES, not line TEXT: an annotation-only rewrite that re-wraps\n   a signature changes every declaration line's text while declaring nothing new. An ANONYMOUS\n   declaration (`export default function (`) yields no name, so it cannot take part in the\n   comparison and is SKIPPED — failing closed rather than firing on a rewrite; a recorded\n   limitation. `tests` is then selected only if NO changed file matches the `tests` globs.\n   Added DECLARATIONS only — branch-line (\"a new `if`/`for`\") detection is explicitly OUT, since\n   no cited corpus case needs it; a follow-up may add it with its own case.\n3. Portable by construction, not by CI: an in-process pure-text scan, identical under the library,\n   CLI and remote-MCP harnesses and under any CI provider or none, assuming NO language and NO\n   repository layout — no `src/**` predicate, no Python-only regex — so it fires for a Ruby, Java,\n   Next.js or .NET target as for this one. Its only path predicates are the `tests` globs and a\n   docs/data extension skip (`.md`/`.rst`/`.txt`/`.json`/`.yaml`/`.yml`/`.toml`/`.cfg`/`.ini`/\n   `.lock`), so a fenced code block in a docs-only change cannot fire it.\n4. A MOVE is not suppressed: the reviewed diff carries no `-M` (`assemble.py:230`), so a rename\n   arrives as delete-in-A + add-in-B whose added names are new to B — 1920 and 1737 trigger.\n5. The scanned text is the COMPOSED context: `overlay_union` receives\n   `steps.assemble_diff.outputs.context` (`gates/code-review.yaml:63`, `:116`) — a changed-files\n   list plus the diff truncated at `DIFF_CHAR_CAP = 60000` (`assemble.py:30`,\n   `compose_diff_context:77-92`). Both shapes are tested; a declaration past the cap does not\n   trigger (a recorded blind spot), while `changed_files` is truncation-proof — hence keying the\n   rule's negative half on it. Accepted consequences: the `tests`-keyed Pass-4 coach moves\n   (`moves.py:30`, `:75-82`) now offer on ordinary source changes, and each qualifying change\n   costs one extra uncapped Round-A overlay call.\n6. Narrow guard (vii) in `code-review-tests.md:97` so a coverage gap is blocking-eligible only when\n   the finding concerns *newly introduced behaviour* AND names a *violating input*. No trigger\n   provenance reaches the reviewer (`workflow_ops.py:61-65` supplies context/changed_files/\n   scope_context only), so the prompt DEFINES both from the diff alone: behaviour is *newly\n   introduced* when its implementing lines appear as ADDED (`+`) lines in the diff under review;\n   behaviour reachable only through unadded lines is *pre-existing* and stays advisory; a *named\n   violating input* is a concrete value, argument or call sequence stated verbatim in the finding\n   that the added lines mishandle. ONE prompt serves both rounds, so this also narrows blocking\n   eligibility for the existing glob-triggered population (0.54, 8.0% of changes) — intended; the\n   corpus gains the pre-existing FP case.\n\n## Scope\nUnder `src/rebar/llm/code_review/`: `registry.py` (594 lines, cap 800) — `changed_files` kwarg +\n`_has_added_declaration`; `workflow_ops.py` — pass `changed` at `:99`, refresh the step\n`description=` (`:70-86`); `criteria_routing.json` — `tests` `_comment` only, `applies_to`\nunchanged. Also `reviewers/code-review-tests.md` (guard (vii)) and\n`eval_specs/code-review-tests.eval.yaml` (one recall + one FP case), both under `src/rebar/llm/`;\nnew `tests/unit/test_code_review_tests_trigger.py` (`tests/unit/llm/` does not exist).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\nUnless stated: `pytest tests/unit/test_code_review_tests_trigger.py -q`.\n- [ ] A diff whose only addition is a function/method/class declaration, with no changed test file,\n  selects `tests` from `content_triggered_overlays` and, through `overlay_union`, sets\n  `include_tests` true — asserted on BOTH a raw diff and its `compose_diff_context` output.\n- [ ] Parametrised replay over synthetic diffs of each cited shape: SELECT for `d9e0f0e733` (new\n  module, 15 added declarations), `721cb7d6ac` (moved declarations, 3), `1c2bf6e57b` (module split,\n  22); DO NOT SELECT for `24917e843b` (comment-only source delta, 0 added declarations) or\n  `83d91d488c` (annotation-only rewrite; all 8 added names also removed in the same file). An\n  added ANONYMOUS declaration selects nothing.\n- [ ] Portability: an added declaration in a non-Python, non-`src/` file — a Go `func` under\n  `internal/`, a TS `export function` under `app/`, a Ruby `class` under `lib/` — selects `tests`,\n  while a docs-only diff whose markdown fence holds `def f():` and a config-only diff do NOT.\n- [ ] A test-file-only diff does not content-select it; a declaration *plus* a `tests/` file still\n  selects it by glob (union, not replacement).\n- [ ] Guard (vii) defines newly-introduced-vs-pre-existing behaviour and \"named violating input\"\n  in diff-decidable terms, with the Four-Criterion Test bullet unchanged; asserted via\n  `prompts.get_prompt(\"code-review-tests\")`.\n- [ ] The `overlay_union` step's `description=` names the added-declaration content trigger, not\n  deletion-impact as the only one.\n- [ ] No regression, no baseline edit: `pytest tests/unit/test_code_review_triggers.py\n  tests/unit/test_code_review_deletion_impact.py tests/unit/test_code_review_evals.py\n  tests/unit/test_criteria_vocabulary_reviewers.py -q` green with the first two unedited (35 tests),\n  `python scripts/check_criteria_vocabulary.py` clean, and `check_complexity_baseline.py --check`\n  reporting `new=0 increased=0` with `registry.py` under `.github/module-size-limit.txt`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787378975575766001,"uuid":"0adce9c0-7b10-4c7d-8f5e-11e131d9f223"}